  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes how the histone chaperone FACT helps RNA polymerase II move through chromatin during transcription. Nucleosomes are shown as repeated DNA-wrapped histone particles along a blue DNA strand, while RNA Pol II is the green complex producing a red RNA transcript. FACT, shown in purple, associates with nucleosomes near the advancing polymerase.\n\nAs transcription proceeds, the polymerase encounters a nucleosome barrier. FACT appears to promote partial nucleosome disassembly by interacting with an H2A–H2B dimer, allowing the polymerase to pass while preserving histone components rather than causing complete nucleosome loss. From this intermediate state, the figure shows two possible outcomes. In one, FACT supports reassembly or maintenance of the nucleosome after polymerase passage, preserving chromatin structure. In the other, a histone methyltransferase (HMT) methylates H3 at Gln105, and this modification inhibits FACT’s activity. When FACT is blocked by this methylation, nucleosome reorganization appears altered, favoring a more open or disrupted chromatin state around the transcribed region.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights a balance between transcription through nucleosomes, FACT-dependent chromatin restoration, and inhibition of FACT by histone modification.",
